From 25bf49a95249782311a3d88b2246fb571227e6ed Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <claude@example.com>
Date: Wed, 8 Jul 2026 08:34:40 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] COB interpreter: jump-table opcode dispatch (was GCC
 comparison tree)

CCobThread::Tick dispatched 64 sparse 0x1000_xxxx opcodes via switch(opcode),
which GCC compiled to a ~6-deep comparison tree (13 cmp + range branches, no
jump table - confirmed by disassembly). Dispatch instead on (opcode >> 12),
whose values (0x10001..0x100A2) are dense enough that GCC emits a real jump
table (1 indirect jmp, zero 0x1000xxxx compares in the new disassembly). The
4 opcode groups sharing a >>12 key (PUSH_CONSTANT/LOCAL_VAR/STATIC;
POP_LOCAL_VAR/STATIC; DIV/MOD; CALL/REAL_CALL/LUA_CALL/BATCH_LUA) get an inner
switch(opcode); the single CALL->REAL_CALL fall-through and every in-case
break/return are preserved (an inner break exits to the outer case's break =
same net control flow). Value-identical dispatch; byte-identical output.

Measured (bench, medium [6000,9000), wt=1, min-of-3): 34.405e9 -> 34.245e9
instr = -0.46%, byte-identical. Main-thread (CCobEngine::Tick runs on the sim
thread), so unlike the QTPFS wins this one can help wt=2 wall. Dispatch was a
smaller share of Tick than estimated (handlers dominate), so this is the
ceiling for the dispatch lever. (Case-body indentation is transformer-
generated; semantics unchanged.)
